1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:07,590
>> At 3,000 rpm, a car engine's crankshaft turns 50 times a second.

2
00:00:07,670 --> 00:00:13,740
>> And yet each cylinder only burns fuel 25 times a second. Once every two turns.

3
00:00:13,820 --> 00:00:20,260
Its piston makes four trips for every burn, and only one of those trips actually pushes.

4
00:00:20,340 --> 00:00:23,580
>> Four trips, one push. It sounds wasteful.

5
00:00:23,660 --> 00:00:27,950
>> It does. And it's still the design inside most car engines.

6
00:00:28,030 --> 00:00:33,490
>> It's the four-stroke cycle. It was first patented by a man who never built one

7
00:00:33,570 --> 00:00:39,690
>> and made to work by a former grocery salesman, who later lost his patent.

8
00:00:42,290 --> 00:00:47,730
>> Let's open one up. This is a simplified diagram of a single cylinder.

9
00:00:47,810 --> 00:00:51,820
>> So inside the cylinder there's a piston, sliding up and down.

10
00:00:51,900 --> 00:00:56,590
>> And a connecting rod links that piston to the crankshaft, which turns.

11
00:00:56,670 --> 00:00:59,280
>> So up-and-down becomes round-and-round.

12
00:00:59,360 --> 00:01:06,550
>> Exactly. Up top there are two valves: one lets air and fuel in, the other lets burnt gas out.

13
00:01:06,630 --> 00:01:11,240
>> And cams on a camshaft push them open, then springs close them again.

14
00:01:11,320 --> 00:01:14,260
>> Between them sits the spark plug.

15
00:01:14,340 --> 00:01:20,160
>> The piston's highest point is called top dead centre, and its lowest, bottom dead centre.

16
00:01:20,240 --> 00:01:25,780
>> At each of them the piston is momentarily at rest, before it turns around.

17
00:01:25,860 --> 00:01:29,450
>> And one trip between them, up or down, is a stroke.

18
00:01:29,530 --> 00:01:36,400
>> Now watch the piston as the crank turns. It follows along, but not in a perfectly even rhythm.

19
00:01:36,480 --> 00:01:38,790
>> It looks like a smooth wave to me.

20
00:01:38,870 --> 00:01:44,590
>> Nearly. But after a quarter turn, the piston has already covered more than half its stroke.

21
00:01:44,670 --> 00:01:47,490
>> How can a quarter turn give you more than half?

22
00:01:47,570 --> 00:01:55,840
>> The rod tilts. That makes the piston hurry through the top half of the stroke, and dawdle through the bottom.

23
00:01:59,640 --> 00:02:03,360
>> Okay. Let's run one whole cycle, slowly, from the top.

24
00:02:03,440 --> 00:02:10,380
>> Stroke 1: intake. The intake valve opens, and the falling piston draws in air and fuel.

25
00:02:10,460 --> 00:02:13,780
>> Pulled in by the partial vacuum it leaves behind.

26
00:02:13,860 --> 00:02:21,380
Stroke 2: compression. Both valves shut, and the rising piston squeezes the mixture into about a tenth of the space.

27
00:02:21,460 --> 00:02:25,870
>> And by now the crankshaft has turned once, all the way round.

28
00:02:25,950 --> 00:02:30,390
>> Near the top, the spark plug fires, and the mixture starts to burn.

29
00:02:30,470 --> 00:02:35,710
>> Stroke 3: power. The hot, high-pressure gas drives the piston down.

30
00:02:35,790 --> 00:02:38,950
>> And that's the only stroke that pushes on the crank.

31
00:02:39,030 --> 00:02:43,250
>> The other three just get the gas in, ready, and out again.

32
00:02:43,330 --> 00:02:50,820
>> Stroke 4: exhaust. The exhaust valve opens, and the rising piston pushes the burnt gas out.

33
00:02:50,900 --> 00:02:55,990
>> Then it all starts again. Four strokes, two turns of the crank, one push.

34
00:02:56,070 --> 00:03:01,940
>> Which is why the camshaft turns at half the crankshaft's speed: each valve opens once per cycle.

35
00:03:02,020 --> 00:03:05,760
>> Two turns of the crank, one turn of the cam.

36
00:03:05,840 --> 00:03:09,680
>> But what carries the piston through the three strokes that don't push?

37
00:03:09,760 --> 00:03:18,400
>> A heavy flywheel. It soaks up energy from each power stroke, and gives some back to squeeze the next charge.

38
00:03:22,000 --> 00:03:27,660
So who came up with all this? The idea is older than the engine that made it famous.

39
00:03:27,740 --> 00:03:35,310
>> In 1860, Étienne Lenoir in Paris built a gas engine that burned its fuel without compressing it first.

40
00:03:35,390 --> 00:03:40,010
>> And it turned only about 4% of the fuel's energy into work.

41
00:03:40,090 --> 00:03:43,410
>> Four percent. So almost all of it went to waste.

42
00:03:43,490 --> 00:03:52,130
>> Almost all. Then a travelling salesman in Germany, Nicolaus Otto, learned of it, and started building engines himself.

43
00:03:52,210 --> 00:03:59,900
>> In 1862 he tried one that compressed its charge first. It ran for a few minutes, then destroyed itself.

44
00:03:59,980 --> 00:04:07,920
>> That same year, in France, Alphonse Beau de Rochas patented the four-stroke cycle. But he never built an engine.

45
00:04:08,000 --> 00:04:12,290
>> So the idea existed on paper before anyone made it run.

46
00:04:12,370 --> 00:04:20,360
>> Meanwhile, in 1864, Otto and Eugen Langen founded the first company devoted to making engines.

47
00:04:20,440 --> 00:04:28,330
>> Their atmospheric engine won a gold medal at the 1867 Paris World's Fair, at about 12% efficiency.

48
00:04:28,410 --> 00:04:30,650
>> Three times Lenoir's.

49
00:04:30,730 --> 00:04:37,500
>> Then, in 1876, Otto's company built an engine that compressed its charge in four strokes.

50
00:04:37,580 --> 00:04:45,170
>> It was an immediate success: the first commercially successful engine to compress its charge in the cylinder.

51
00:04:45,250 --> 00:04:53,020
>> And two of Otto's former engineers, Gottlieb Daimler and Wilhelm Maybach, made it small and fast enough for vehicles.

52
00:04:53,100 --> 00:05:01,060
>> But in 1886 the patent was cancelled in Germany, because Beau de Rochas had patented the cycle first.

53
00:05:01,140 --> 00:05:05,580
>> So a patent for an engine nobody had built ended Otto's monopoly.

54
00:05:05,660 --> 00:05:10,480
>> And neither Otto nor Daimler had even known that patent existed.

55
00:05:10,560 --> 00:05:17,520
>> Still, the engines spread. By 1889, more than 50 companies were building Otto-type engines.

56
00:05:17,600 --> 00:05:22,970
>> And the cycle still carries Otto's name: the Otto cycle.

57
00:05:26,570 --> 00:05:32,810
>> So why does squeezing first help so much? Engineers answer with a graph of pressure against volume.

58
00:05:32,890 --> 00:05:38,550
>> This is the idealised version, the Otto cycle, traced as our engine turns.

59
00:05:38,630 --> 00:05:42,600
>> Intake: the volume grows, at about the pressure outside.

60
00:05:42,680 --> 00:05:47,870
>> Compression: the volume shrinks tenfold, and the pressure climbs steeply.

61
00:05:47,950 --> 00:05:54,390
>> Combustion is treated as instant, right at the top: the pressure leaps while the volume stays the same.

62
00:05:54,470 --> 00:05:59,490
>> In a real engine the burn takes a little time, but this shape is the point.

63
00:05:59,570 --> 00:06:04,310
Power: the hot gas expands and pushes, and its pressure falls.

64
00:06:04,390 --> 00:06:08,800
>> At the bottom, the exhaust valve opens, and the pressure drops back.

65
00:06:08,880 --> 00:06:14,000
>> Exhaust: the piston sweeps the gas out, and the loop closes.

66
00:06:14,080 --> 00:06:16,320
>> And what does the loop tell us?

67
00:06:16,400 --> 00:06:24,640
>> The area inside it is the net work: what the power stroke gave, minus what the compression cost.

68
00:06:24,720 --> 00:06:31,220
>> So squeezing costs some work, but the hot gas pays back much more on the way down.

69
00:06:33,820 --> 00:06:40,640
>> Which brings us to the compression ratio: the cylinder's largest volume divided by its smallest.

70
00:06:40,720 --> 00:06:45,410
>> And in the ideal cycle, the efficiency depends on that ratio alone.

71
00:06:45,490 --> 00:06:53,330
>> Efficiency is 1 minus r to the power 1 minus γ, where γ, gamma, is 1.4 for air.

72
00:06:53,410 --> 00:06:55,230
>> Let's put some numbers in.

73
00:06:55,310 --> 00:07:00,880
Squeeze four to one, and the ideal engine turns 43% of its heat into work.

74
00:07:00,960 --> 00:07:07,220
>> At ten to one, 60%. At sixteen to one, 67%.

75
00:07:07,300 --> 00:07:10,870
>> Sixty percent? Car engines don't get anywhere near that.

76
00:07:10,950 --> 00:07:19,270
>> No. Real engines lose heat and friction along the way: a petrol engine turns about 30% into useful work.

77
00:07:19,350 --> 00:07:24,370
>> But the lesson holds: squeeze harder, and you get more work from the same fuel.

78
00:07:24,450 --> 00:07:29,640
>> So why do most petrol car engines stop at around 8 to 12 to one?

79
00:07:29,720 --> 00:07:34,660
>> Because squeezing a gas heats it, and a hot enough mixture can ignite by itself.

80
00:07:34,740 --> 00:07:39,400
>> Normally, the spark starts a flame front that sweeps across the chamber.

81
00:07:39,480 --> 00:07:45,370
>> But if the unburnt mixture ahead of the flame gets too hot, pockets of it explode all at once.

82
00:07:45,450 --> 00:07:50,790
>> That's knock: a metallic pinging, and pressure spikes that can damage the engine.

83
00:07:50,870 --> 00:07:54,160
>> So it's knock, not the formula, that sets the limit.

84
00:07:54,240 --> 00:08:02,730
>> Right. A fuel's octane rating measures how well it resists igniting by itself. Higher octane allows more squeeze.

85
00:08:02,810 --> 00:08:09,910
>> And modern engines listen for knock with sensors, and fire the spark later when they hear it.

86
00:08:13,510 --> 00:08:19,880
So far, everything has happened exactly at the top or bottom of a stroke. Real engines don't wait.

87
00:08:19,960 --> 00:08:26,100
>> The spark fires early: some 10 to 40 degrees of crank rotation before the top.

88
00:08:26,180 --> 00:08:29,120
>> Before the piston has even finished squeezing?

89
00:08:29,200 --> 00:08:35,290
>> Yes. The flame takes time to burn, and the peak pressure should come just after the top.

90
00:08:35,370 --> 00:08:41,830
>> And the flame doesn't speed up with the engine, so at higher revs the spark needs a longer head start.

91
00:08:41,910 --> 00:08:47,820
>> The valves are early, too. They open before the end of a stroke, and close after it.

92
00:08:47,900 --> 00:08:53,420
>> Which means that at the end of the exhaust stroke, both valves are briefly open at once.

93
00:08:53,500 --> 00:08:57,490
>> That's valve overlap, and it's designed in on purpose.

94
00:08:57,570 --> 00:08:59,560
>> Why would you want both open?

95
00:08:59,640 --> 00:09:05,630
>> The outgoing exhaust is moving fast, and it helps draw the fresh charge in behind it.

96
00:09:05,710 --> 00:09:11,070
>> So the trick is to close the exhaust valve just as the fresh charge reaches it.

97
00:09:11,150 --> 00:09:21,360
>> Exactly. Racing engines use lots of overlap. Road engines, which must idle smoothly below 1,000 rpm, use less.

98
00:09:21,440 --> 00:09:28,910
>> And many now shift their camshafts as they run, to get both: power at high revs, and a smooth idle.

99
00:09:32,510 --> 00:09:38,670
>> One cylinder gives one push every two turns. That's a lumpy way to drive a car.

100
00:09:38,750 --> 00:09:44,040
>> So most car engines have several cylinders on one crankshaft, firing in turn.

101
00:09:44,120 --> 00:09:51,780
>> Spaced evenly, the power strokes come every 720 degrees divided by the number of cylinders.

102
00:09:51,860 --> 00:09:57,500
>> With four cylinders, that's every 180 degrees: a push every half turn.

103
00:09:57,580 --> 00:10:03,250
>> And a straight four usually fires its cylinders in the order 1, 3, 4, 2.

104
00:10:03,330 --> 00:10:05,370
>> Not one, two, three, four?

105
00:10:05,450 --> 00:10:12,810
>> One, three, four, two is the usual order. Some British engines used one, two, four, three.

106
00:10:12,890 --> 00:10:17,930
>> Here's the twisting force that the gas puts on the crank, in an idealised model.

107
00:10:18,010 --> 00:10:23,900
>> One cylinder: one big shove, then a long wait, with a drag while it compresses.

108
00:10:23,980 --> 00:10:27,070
>> Four cylinders: one shove after another.

109
00:10:27,150 --> 00:10:33,360
>> Six cylinders: the power strokes overlap, and the push is smoother still.

110
00:10:33,440 --> 00:10:38,760
>> And even spacing means less vibration, and a smoother sound.

111
00:10:42,360 --> 00:10:45,800
There's another way to light the fire: no spark at all.

112
00:10:45,880 --> 00:10:52,990
>> A diesel draws in only air, and squeezes it much harder, typically 15 to 23 to one.

113
00:10:53,070 --> 00:10:56,760
>> And squeezing air that hard heats it by hundreds of degrees.

114
00:10:56,840 --> 00:11:02,010
>> Near the top, fuel is sprayed in, and it ignites in the hot air by itself.

115
00:11:02,090 --> 00:11:05,200
>> So the squeeze itself does the spark plug's job.

116
00:11:05,280 --> 00:11:13,020
>> In a way. With no fuel in the cylinder during the squeeze, nothing can ignite early, so it can squeeze harder.

117
00:11:13,100 --> 00:11:19,690
>> That's one reason diesels are efficient. Big, slow ship diesels reach up to 55%.

118
00:11:19,770 --> 00:11:26,130
>> And instead of throttling its air, a diesel sets its power by how much fuel it injects.

119
00:11:26,210 --> 00:11:36,850
>> Rudolf Diesel's first prototype fired in 1893. His third, tested in 1897, turned about 26% of its fuel into work.

120
00:11:36,930 --> 00:11:43,370
>> And it runs the same four strokes: intake, compression, power, exhaust.

121
00:11:46,970 --> 00:11:49,390
>> So let's put the whole idea together.

122
00:11:49,470 --> 00:11:54,560
>> Draw in, squeeze, burn, push out: four strokes, two turns of the crank.

123
00:11:54,640 --> 00:11:58,830
>> The squeeze makes it efficient, and knock sets the limit on the squeeze.

124
00:11:58,910 --> 00:12:06,800
>> Patented in 1862 by a man who never built one, made to work by Otto in 1876

125
00:12:06,880 --> 00:12:10,390
>> it's still the cycle inside most car engines.

126
00:12:10,470 --> 00:12:16,080
Strange to think all of that is happening under the bonnet, every time a car idles.

127
00:12:16,160 --> 00:12:24,760
>> At 3,000 rpm, that's two turns and one push, 25 times every second.
